    "math_academy_id": "q-104787",
    "knowledge_point_id": "aeaff08f-f2c5-5e51-baca-988348852948",
    "knowledge_point": "Computing the Derivative of a Linear Sum of Functions",
    "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/e02f873d2d583767e0b29e7ad57121e23e5c73716ce058ef6bed09b644b37bce.png)\n\nThe graphs of the functions $y=f(x)$ and $y=g(x)$ are given above. Find ${h}^{′}(-1)$ where $h(x)=2f(x)+g(x).$",
    "worked_solution": "The addition and constant multiple rules for derivatives give\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(x)=2{f}^{′}(x)+{g}^{′}(x).\n$$\n\nAs a result, we have that\n\n$$\n{h}^{′}(-1)=2{f}^{′}(-1)+{g}^{′}(-1).\n$$\n\nAlso, notice that $x=-1$ corresponds to\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-4,0)$ for the function $f,$ and\n\n- the interval $x\\in (-2,1)$ for the function $g,$\n\nas shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13934289/assets/f64bb3d174950f3c4ac26d7d65d0b3b7881761fa3fb2ddd166f3a23c4af7fa27.png)\n\n- The slope of $y=f(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-4,0)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δf}{Δx}=\\frac{0-4}{0-(-4)}=-1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${f}^{′}(-1)=-1.$\n\n- The slope of $y=g(x)$ is constant on $x\\in (-2,1)$ and equals\n\n$$\n\\frac{Δg}{Δx}=\\frac{2-4}{1-(-2)}=-\\frac{2}{3}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${g}^{′}(-1)=-\\frac{2}{3}.$\n\nFinally, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{′}(-1) & =2{f}^{′}(-1)+{g}^{′}(-1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-1)-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =-\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
